{
 "id": "safety",
 "name": "Safety",
 "summary": "Driver protection for a bare-chassis Gen 6 Camaro road-race car: a weld-in 1.75 x .120 DOM roll cage tied into the unibody, FIA/SFI fixed-back seat, 6-point harness with FHR-compatible geometry, SFI 27.1 window net, plumbed Novec fire system plus hand-held extinguisher, external kill/fire pulls, sealed fuel-cell and engine firewalls, and marked tow points. The production car has none of this; every item is a race addition built to NASA CCR / SCCA GCR (and FIA App. J 253 where applicable).",
 "overview": "The cage is the structural and safety core of the car. A Gen 6 Camaro races at roughly 3,400–3,700 lb without driver and fuel, putting it in NASA's 3,001–4,000 lb tube bracket (1.75 x .120 DOM or chromoly, no ERW) as quoted from the CCR; SCCA's 2,700 lb+ table allows 1.50 x .120, 1.75 x .095 or 2.00 x .080 (no mixing alloy and mild steel, inspection holes in front/main hoop and one brace). FIA 253-8 (2020) build-to-spec uses 45 x 2.5 or 50 x 2.0 mm for the main hoop and 38 x 2.5 / 40 x 2.0 mm elsewhere, ≥3 mm/120 cm² reinforcement plates, and U-gussets ≥1 mm thick whose legs end 2–4 x OD from the junction.\n\nBuild sequence matters: strip and seam-weld the shell, mock up seat, steering column and pedals, then set hoop positions around the seated driver in full gear. The harness bar height is set from the seated driver so shoulder belts leave the shoulders at 0° to −20° (Schroth/FIA guidance; never upward with an FHR), and the seat back sits within ~200 mm of the bar. Lap belts anchor at 20°–70° on 7/16-20 eyebolts (40 N·m per Schroth) into ≥40 cm² x 3 mm plates; belts may never anchor to the seat or seat mounts.\n\nFire: a 10 lb SFI 17.1 or FIA-listed Novec 1230 system with 2 cockpit nozzles under the dash (aimed at footwell/midriff, never the face) and 3 engine-bay nozzles between mid-height and top of the engine. Tubing is the maker's homologated semi-rigid alloy tube with push-in fittings, P-clipped 75–100 mm behind every nozzle. Systems are recertified every 2 years by the maker only. Electrical cutoff hardware belongs to the electrical system; this system owns the mechanical pull cables, markings and decals.\n\nCommon failure points: expired belts/nets/seats (SFI belts and nets 2 years; FIA belts and 8855-1999 seats 5 years; 8862/8855-2021 seats 10 years), cracked floor around cage plates, padding slid off the helmet zone, loose seat bolts, bottle out of service date, and unsealed bulkhead pass-throughs letting fuel vapor into the cockpit.",
 "buildNotes": [
  "Strip interior to bare shell; remove SRS/airbags and factory belts (interior.strip-and-deadener-removal).",
  "Mock up seat, steering column, pedal box and driver in full gear before designing the cage.",
  "Weld mounting plates, then main hoop, front hoop, roof rails, roof X, diagonals, harness bar, backstays, door bars, dash bar, optional front down-bars; gusset junctions.",
  "Drill inspection holes, weld all tabs (seat brace, window net rods, dash, fire bottle, extinguisher) BEFORE paint/powder coat.",
  "Install bulkheads (rear fuel-cell firewall, package shelf, front block-offs) and seal every pass-through.",
  "Install seat mounts + reinforcement plates, harness anchors and plates; fit seat and harness; verify belt angles with driver.",
  "Install fire system bottle/bracket, tubing, nozzles, pull cables; hand-held extinguisher; kill-switch pull cable.",
  "Fit SFI 45.1 padding, window net, decals and tow straps last; record all expiry dates in the logbook."
 ],
